WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:03.600
Hello everyone
and welcome back to EU People.

00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:05.800
Today we have Jean-Éric Paquet with us.

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:08.240
The science supremo
of the European Commission.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:12.800
Together we will see if Europe is
staying ahead in the innovation race.

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:15.320
Jean-Éric is also a jack of all trades.

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:18.720
You will find his finger prints
on many EU initiatives.

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:22.760
Then, we will try to confront
Jean-Éric with his own creation, Nao,

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:24.640
a little brainy robot.

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:27.120
So, whether you’re bats
about biotechnology

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:30.840
or nuts for nuclear physics,
strap in, the show is for you.

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:45.960
I am delighted
to welcome Jean-Éric Paquet,

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:50.280
the Chief of the Research and Innovation
Department at the European Commission.

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:53.680
Together with his team,
Jean-Éric helps researchers,

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:55.840
push the boundaries of knowledge.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:01.120
Watch out, the man heads
a 77-billion-euro research program.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:03.040
What does he do with all this money?

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:04.640
Let's find out with our guest.

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:05.960
Good morning Jean-Éric.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.040
Romain, good morning.

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:12.280
So technically, are you a billionaire?

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:14.800
Well that’s a nice way of putting it,

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:16.640
I don’t think
I’ve ever looked at it like that.

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:20.760
I mean, it’s the biggest
research programme in the world,

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:23.840
77 billion euros, that’s Horizon 2020,

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:26.800
and in fact we are even more even
more ambitious for the next programme,

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:29.600
Horizon Europe will be 100 billion euros

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:33.160
if we are followed by finance ministers
and the European parliament.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:35.480
So, yes, it’s a very very big programme,

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:39.240
one which allows Europe
to invest into technologies,

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:42.320
into new science, new knowledge,
into the future really,

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:46.080
and in fact the billionaires
for me are not commission officials,

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:49.000
its really the scientists
and the citizens

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:51.680
which benefit
from this quite amazing programme.

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:54.240
I found some amazing pictures of you

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:57.040
in your daily work
and we’re going to see some,

00:01:57.080 --> 00:02:00.520
it seems it’s quite fun
to lead research in Europe,

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:04.320
can you tell us a bit more
about your daily life?

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:10.480
Well, uh,
I mean my day starts on my bike in fact,

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:14.120
I bike into the office in the morning,
which gives me I think a good sense

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:17.560
of how much more we need to do
in the urban context.

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:18.880
On pollution?

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:21.920
Pollution,
also the way our cities are organised,

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:23.640
we do a lot of research on that.

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:27.600
Then I spend my day,
I´m extraordinarily privileged,

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:32.560
because on the one hand I meet only
very remarkably intelligent

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:35.960
and creative and smart people,
I mean all scientists,

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:38.960
so I learn absolutely every day,

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.680
and that is I think quite
a privileged position,

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:43.160
to work and learn.

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:48.120
Then I of course also do quite
a lot of more institutional work,

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:53.760
and right now a lot of my daily work is
about negotiating the future programme,

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:56.600
the one I mentioned,
Horizon Europe, 100 billion euros.

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:01.240
So, I’m travelling across Europe to meet
ministers and administrations

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:03.480
and also those
which will do the programme

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:05.240
with us to prepare the ground.

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:10.360
Can we look at concrete examples
from the Horizon 2020?

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:13.720
So, I’ve read in your brochure

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:17.920
that the EU helped to discover
7 planets, is it true?

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:20.480
Yes, yes yes, it is true,
very true, it was last year.

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:23.240
That’s again, I mentioned
the European Research Council,

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:27.440
this flagship European institution,
this noble prize factory.

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:29.640
It was funded by the ERC,

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:34.720
the ERC is a very important ground
which allows research teams in all areas,

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:37.600
this was astrophysics,
to carry out their research.

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:41.240
It’s a researcher from Liege,
so here from Belgium

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:43.160
which I find also very nice,

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:47.360
he used a telescope which is partly
European called Trapiste,

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:51.960
and his project was called Speculoos
which I think is also quite attractive,

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:55.240
and he indeed discovered 7 exo-planets,

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:57.480
which are these planets
outside the solar system,

00:03:57.520 --> 00:03:59.640
these are not the first ones discovered

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:02.720
but any new discovery adds
of course knowledge

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:06.000
and maybe the perspective
for life beyond the solar system.

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:09.560
I’ve read also
that the EU funded research

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:13.800
on the DNA of a plant
and it saved a 7-year-old boy?

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:18.560
Yeah, he was,
most of his skin was disappearing,

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:23.840
and this was, again, an example
of a particularly rare disease,

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:25.800
a genetic disease.

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:30.520
Here we crossed disciplinary knowledge
and disciplinary science

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:35.560
and indeed the research project allowed
to provide a solution

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:39.840
and this kid is recovering
which is absolutely amazing.

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:42.240
Also, is it true
that the EU is helping to find

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:44.880
a sustainable alternative to plastics?

00:04:44.920 --> 00:04:46.600
We need to provide alternatives to it,

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:50.640
and indeed we have funded,
I mean I can show you here,

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:56.520
this for example is a small pot
for cosmetics, which is,

00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:59.800
which used to be often in plastic,

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:03.240
this one has exactly the same qualities,

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:09.040
the same properties but it degrades
in nature in a few weeks’ time,

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:11.520
which is really very remarkable.

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:14.080
This is a company in a Member State

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:18.480
which was indeed funded under
one of our instruments, the concept,

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:20.280
the innovation was there

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:23.200
but what they didn’t have
was the capacity to scale it up,

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:25.800
to make it
a real industrial possibility.

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:29.280
They are there now, now of course they
need to become a commercial success,

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:31.200
that’s not up to us,
that’s up to the company,

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:32.840
but this is really very remarkable.

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:34.960
Here’s another,
it’s all completely plastic

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:37.280
but that’s another nice example
of the circular economy.

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:41.400
This is a coffee cup,
we are still in the morning

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:43.160
so I will have a sip of it,

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:47.520
and this cup is made of coffee.

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:49.520
Itself?

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:52.400
The cup itself is coffee,
compressed coffee residue-

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:53.600
-coffee waste?

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:56.520
Exactly, coffee residues
from expresso machines,

00:05:56.560 --> 00:06:01.080
it is compressed into this very
nice looking small cup.

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:03.040
So, the tabs
that you have in your coffee machine

00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:05.080
that you throw away
are recycled to make this?

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:06.160
They can be recycled.

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:16.120
Jean-Éric,
you are a real jack of all trades.

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:17.360
Before going into research,

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:19.280
you worked in the Secretariat-General
of the Commission.

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:22.880
Among other things, you are behind
the European Solidarity Corps.

00:06:22.920 --> 00:06:25.960
At first it was Jean-Claude Juncker,
the President of the Commission,

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:29.200
who announced the creation
of a European Solidarity Corps

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:32.400
in his State
of the Union address in 2016.

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:35.640
The idea is to create
a European programme

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:37.960
that allows young Europeans
up to 30 years old

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:41.400
to participate in solidarity activities

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:43.560
in the member states
of the European Union.

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:45.480
It’s not an entirely new idea.

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:49.200
There have been European volunteers
for two decades now,

00:06:49.240 --> 00:06:54.480
but the President's idea was really
to cover more sectors,

00:06:54.520 --> 00:06:58.000
all Member States, maybe older people,
to really allow them to demonstrate

00:06:58.040 --> 00:07:04.040
their concern
and their wish for solidarity.

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:09.240
The idea is to propose to 100.000
young Europeans from now to 2020

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:13.520
to spend a few months up
to one year in a member state,

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:19.160
often another member state,
to help in the social sector,

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:21.640
the health sector,
the humanitarian sector,

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:25.120
and also perhaps in the environmental
or ecology sector.

00:07:25.160 --> 00:07:27.480
There is huge demand
for young Europeans.

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:29.040
You would have done it too.

00:07:29.080 --> 00:07:32.560
I suggested to my sons to sign up,
I think they signed up.

00:07:32.840 --> 00:07:37.520
I don’t think they were selected,
but they signed up.

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:39.720
Would I have done it if I were young?
I believe so, yes.

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:42.760
A lot of questions come up in Europe,
as you already know,

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:45.560
and we collected
some questions that we found online.

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:47.800
There are concerns, there are hopes.

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:49.480
I’d like to hear your thoughts.

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:53.880
The first one is
on the European social rights framework.

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:56.440
Bruno made this caustic comment:

00:07:56.480 --> 00:07:59.760
“What does the European framework
for social rights bring?

00:07:59.800 --> 00:08:02.080
Short answer: Nothing.”

00:08:02.120 --> 00:08:04.200
Well, I have a somewhat longer answer.

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:07.360
This European framework
for social rights is, first of all,

00:08:07.400 --> 00:08:10.720
about showing Europeans that Europe
is not only the internal market.

00:08:10.760 --> 00:08:12.720
Europe is not just free economy,

00:08:12.760 --> 00:08:16.880
sometimes highly criticized in public
discourse in many Member States.

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:20.720
For the President of the Commission,
for the European institutions,

00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:24.240
the social dimensions and the questions
related to jobs in particular

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:26.880
are also at the heart
of the European project

00:08:26.920 --> 00:08:29.080
and at the heart of the European values.

00:08:29.120 --> 00:08:32.280
To be more specific,
I will give you two measures

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:34.800
that have been agreed upon
by all the institutions.

00:08:34.840 --> 00:08:38.640
The first is a measure that establishes
a minimum wage in Europe.

00:08:38.680 --> 00:08:44.320
This seems very natural for a member
state such as France or Belgium,

00:08:44.360 --> 00:08:47.440
where minimal wages have existed
for a very long time.

00:08:47.480 --> 00:08:49.320
This is not the case
for all Member States,

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:51.040
so there it serves as a reference point.

00:08:51.080 --> 00:08:54.200
So now many Member States are looking
into establishing a minimum wage.

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:57.880
But what’s more important—I’m also
thinking in terms of future policy—

00:08:57.920 --> 00:09:02.600
is the principle
of a universal minimum wage,

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:05.480
which was proposed in this framework,

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:09.240
allowing all European citizens
to live with dignity.

00:09:09.280 --> 00:09:14.520
This is obviously
an extremely ambitious concept

00:09:14.560 --> 00:09:18.560
which is not yet operational
and is currently being tested.

00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:24.400
It is being experimented on in Belgium,
Finland and other Member States

00:09:24.440 --> 00:09:29.600
where in effect we are testing
the implementation of a minimum income.

00:09:29.640 --> 00:09:31.400
I have another comment from Rico,

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:33.600
he shares one of his dreams on Twitter
and I’ll read it to you.

00:09:33.640 --> 00:09:35.520
I think you’ll like this one:

00:09:35.560 --> 00:09:39.160
“Could we imagine a train line
that connects the Netherlands to Spain,

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:42.600
France to Hungary,
passing through the north of Italy,

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:47.320
Greece to Bulgaria,
Romania, Slovakia, Poland and Estonia?

00:09:47.360 --> 00:09:49.880
A train whose name
would be ‘European Union’?"

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:54.200
That’s a beautiful dream,
which is partly a reality.

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:59.000
What our reader
is suggesting is almost a network.

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:02.520
It exists in part,
we have this European transport network,

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:05.520
this European mobility network
which exists today,

00:10:05.560 --> 00:10:08.200
at least in European policies,

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:11.440
which was implemented
in 2013 and which has,

00:10:11.480 --> 00:10:13.240
first of all,
an infrastructure component,

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:15.400
as trains do have to circulate on rails

00:10:15.440 --> 00:10:19.000
and often the difficulty is
to connect the national networks.

00:10:19.040 --> 00:10:22.640
So that is the European policy,
to construct infrastructures connecting

00:10:22.680 --> 00:10:25.800
the national networks,
the big tunnels such as the Brenner

00:10:25.840 --> 00:10:27.360
or the Lyon-Turin in France.

00:10:27.400 --> 00:10:32.520
But it’s also about having
trains moving on these rails,

00:10:32.560 --> 00:10:34.680
and in my opinion
that's the real challenge,

00:10:34.720 --> 00:10:39.160
finding railroad operators
offering these services.

00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:42.320
The creation of a rail network
in Europe would make that happen.

00:10:42.360 --> 00:10:44.920
There are obviously existing services.

00:10:44.960 --> 00:10:48.680
Perhaps we have this image
of the Orient Express

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:54.360
or these large night trains
which are a collective European fantasy,

00:10:54.400 --> 00:10:56.760
and which I believe
are behind this question.

00:10:56.800 --> 00:11:00.040
And it’s obviously the operators
who first have to propose this to us,

00:11:00.080 --> 00:11:02.760
it is not the Commission
that will implement railroad services,

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:05.320
but I admit that this is a dream
that I will gladly share.

00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:07.080
And it’s also shared a little

00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:08.720
by the European Parliament
and the Commission,

00:11:08.760 --> 00:11:15.400
given that the Parliament proposed
to implement this Interrail,

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:20.680
this railroad pass, DiscoverEu,
to propose to each European citizen

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:26.640
that reaches the age of majority to have
this railroad pass for a few months,

00:11:26.680 --> 00:11:29.480
allowing him to travel in Europe.

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:31.560
I don't know how old you are, Romain...

00:11:31.600 --> 00:11:34.920
Too old.
-So maybe too old for the pass today

00:11:34.960 --> 00:11:38.360
and maybe too young to have used
Interrail in your youth,

00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:39.680
but it's true that in my generation,

00:11:39.720 --> 00:11:42.560
we all had an Interrail pass
that we bought,

00:11:42.600 --> 00:11:46.560
and we all travelled through Europe.

00:11:46.600 --> 00:11:48.240
And you exchanged currencies?

00:11:48.280 --> 00:11:51.440
It was amazing. I went down
to Greece from Strasbourg

00:11:51.480 --> 00:11:53.360
when I was 15 or 16 years old.

00:11:53.400 --> 00:11:54.880
We passed through Vienna,

00:11:54.920 --> 00:11:57.840
and when we arrived
at the border with Slovenia,

00:11:57.880 --> 00:12:01.400
then Croatia and after that Belgrade,
it was an extraordinary adventure.

00:12:01.440 --> 00:12:04.240
At the time, we used to travel
to Yugoslavia without a visa.

00:12:04.280 --> 00:12:06.640
It was truly remarkable.

00:12:14.120 --> 00:12:16.760
German now, so we will have spoken

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:20.440
all the working languages
of the European Union.

00:12:20.480 --> 00:12:21.760
I would like to try.

00:12:21.800 --> 00:12:23.240
You are from Strasbourg, right?

00:12:23.280 --> 00:12:28.360
My parents met in Paris.
In the early sixties.

00:12:28.400 --> 00:12:32.200
But there is a long family history
associated with that.

00:12:32.240 --> 00:12:35.200
My mother had a grandfather.

00:12:35.240 --> 00:12:38.440
He served in the military in Strasbourg.

00:12:38.480 --> 00:12:41.960
At the beginning of the 20th century.
Strasbourg was German then.

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:44.240
He was quite a Francophile.

00:12:44.280 --> 00:12:48.880
My grandmother
on my father’s side was also German.

00:12:49.360 --> 00:12:51.160
And then she became French.

00:12:51.200 --> 00:12:55.600
So, there was already a bit of German
and a bit of French in both families.

00:12:55.640 --> 00:12:59.280
And my parents met in Paris
at the beginning of the 60s,

00:12:59.320 --> 00:13:00.600
which of course is totally romantic.

00:13:01.120 --> 00:13:04.360
And then they had three children.

00:13:04.400 --> 00:13:08.000
As children, we lived in Darmstadt,
Germany for a few years.

00:13:08.040 --> 00:13:10.600
That's why we all speak a little German.

00:13:10.640 --> 00:13:14.400
And then we grew up in Strasbourg.

00:13:14.440 --> 00:13:17.160
You are a true child
of the Elysée Treaty!

00:13:17.200 --> 00:13:22.960
Well, the Elysée Treaty is from 1962.
That is when my parents married.

00:13:23.000 --> 00:13:26.840
My sister was born in ‘64,
and I a bit later.

00:13:26.880 --> 00:13:31.160
Sometimes other Europeans have
some difficulty understanding

00:13:31.200 --> 00:13:34.720
the significance of the special
German-French friendship.

00:13:34.760 --> 00:13:36.600
Is that important to you?

00:13:36.640 --> 00:13:41.200
I mean, Germany and France,
of course with other member states,

00:13:41.240 --> 00:13:43.640
were there from the very beginning.

00:13:43.680 --> 00:13:49.480
And the European project is a project
that comes from the Second World War.

00:13:49.520 --> 00:13:52.960
And that is why,
with this family history,

00:13:53.000 --> 00:13:57.080
the fact that I am now working
in European politics

00:13:57.120 --> 00:14:01.160
here in Brussels is, of course,
something quite natural and normal.

00:14:01.200 --> 00:14:07.000
But German-French,
that is an important dimension.

00:14:07.040 --> 00:14:08.680
And it definitely still is today.

00:14:08.720 --> 00:14:12.600
And that I can work
with Germans in German.

00:14:12.640 --> 00:14:16.840
And that I can help Germans
and French work together.

00:14:16.880 --> 00:14:19.320
This is also part of my work.
That goes without saying.

00:14:19.360 --> 00:14:23.960
My experience is that if France
and Germany collaborate.

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:28.040
But with the Commission, as well,
and sometimes with other Member States…

00:14:28.080 --> 00:14:32.640
If you build something together
from the start, it has much more impact.

00:14:32.680 --> 00:14:36.760
In closing, a difficult question.
Who do you support in football?

00:14:36.800 --> 00:14:38.920
The French or the German National Team?

00:14:38.960 --> 00:14:41.680
In 2018, it was definitely Les Bleus.

00:14:41.720 --> 00:14:44.960
We have some pictures
that we can show you now.

00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:48.400
These are my sons.
That was a really great year.

00:14:48.440 --> 00:14:50.480
I must say, these days I am lucky.

00:14:50.520 --> 00:14:52.880
I usually have at least two teams
in every tournament.

00:14:52.920 --> 00:14:57.160
So... I almost can't lose.

00:14:57.200 --> 00:15:01.800
We have another picture.
This is you with Sebastian Kurz.

00:15:01.840 --> 00:15:03.320
Oh yes, that's a great picture.

00:15:03.360 --> 00:15:05.920
I was still pretty fit then.

00:15:05.960 --> 00:15:09.680
That was a little over two years ago.

00:15:09.720 --> 00:15:14.880
It was a Balkan summit.
Organized by Austria.

00:15:14.920 --> 00:15:19.760
And there was a game
between the EU and Future EU.

00:15:19.800 --> 00:15:22.360
I think Sebastian Kurz
was Foreign Minister then.

00:15:22.400 --> 00:15:25.840
And I was the director for the Balkans
in the Commission at the time.

00:15:25.880 --> 00:15:27.800
And I played
with the Balkan colleagues.

00:15:27.840 --> 00:15:30.000
It was a friendly match.

00:15:30.040 --> 00:15:32.000
It took place
in the Rapid Wien stadium.

00:15:32.040 --> 00:15:33.520
It was very professional.

00:15:33.560 --> 00:15:36.960
The referee was Johannes Hahn,
the Commissioner,

00:15:37.000 --> 00:15:40.280
and Future EU won 4-2.

00:15:40.320 --> 00:15:42.160
Thank you, Jean-Éric,

00:15:42.200 --> 00:15:45.760
for being with us today
and for answering our questions.

00:15:46.720 --> 00:15:48.200
Thank you so much for watching.

00:15:48.240 --> 00:15:52.040
Now you can comment, watch again,
share with your friends.

00:15:52.080 --> 00:15:56.640
And we still have a little game
to play with Nao.

00:15:56.680 --> 00:15:58.840
You can watch that video here.

00:15:58.880 --> 00:16:03.600
And see you soon
for a new episode of EU People.

